W17 — @-response-file expansion, Windows
-----------------------------------------
  working directory C:\kn168\argprobe, containing
    inject    = "-version"
    @inject   = "-version"
  <java> <jvm> -cp <jar> <main> konanc <token>

  token                exit  stdout                    stderr
  @inject                 0  23 B "Kotlin/Native: 2.4.10"   -
  @.\inject               0  23 B same                      -
  @C:\kn168\argprobe\inject
                          0  23 B same                      -
  C:\kn168\argprobe\@inject
                          1   0 B    error: source entry is not a Kotlin file:
                                     C:/kn168/argprobe/@inject
  .\@inject               1   0 B    same
  @nonexistent            1   0 B    error: you have not specified any
                                     compilation arguments. No output has been
                                     produced.

  Expansion is decided by the FIRST CHARACTER of the argv token; the @ is
  stripped and the remainder is the response-file path, absolute or not. The
  last row shows the missing response file does not itself abort argument
  processing — that run failed only because no argument survived it. macOS
  cycle 2 measured `warning: argfile not found` for the same token alongside
  other arguments, which is the same conclusion: a partial mitigation fails
  silently.


C1 — correction to the cycle-2 macOS process-closure claim
-----------------------------------------------------------
Re-reading the cycle-2 reproduction archive:
  evidence/run7-enumeration.log  seeded from an EMPTY allow file; recorded
    /bin/bash and /usr/bin/xcrun as required; its raw tail additionally shows
      /bin/bash: /usr/bin/xcode-select: Operation not permitted
      /bin/bash: /usr/libexec/PlistBuddy: Operation not permitted
      xcrun: error: can't exec '<Xcode>/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/xcodebuild'
    then stopped, because the xcrun denial message has a different shape than
    the JVM's and the extractor could not parse a program name from it.
  evidence/run8-enumeration.log  seeded with those four; added
      <Xcode>/.../XcodeDefault.xctoolchain/usr/bin/ld
      <Xcode>/.../XcodeDefault.xctoolchain/usr/bin/dsymutil
    and reached SUCCESS at iteration 3.
  evidence/allowed-externals.txt  final content, SIX paths — correct for what
    that file is: the sufficient set for the path that succeeded.
Union observed = 7. Sufficient set = 6. The difference is path-dependent: with
/usr/bin/xcode-select allowed, the compiler does not fall back to
`xcrun xcodebuild -version`. The cycle-2 prose said "exactly seven"; that claim
is WITHDRAWN. No completeness claim is made for macOS. The exclusion does not
need one: the first external executable already violates decision 0008 section 6
item 3, four of the observed paths are absolute OS paths compiled into the
compiler, and the cache-identity ground is independent of the process count.

C2 — method consequence
------------------------
Iterative denial proves "required on the path taken" and "this set is
sufficient". It cannot prove "no other executable is ever spawned". That is
enough to EXCLUDE a tuple and not enough to ADMIT one, so acceptance
requirement A3 now demands a kernel-level trace that records every process
start by resolved image path, and names iterative denial as inadmissible for
admission. W13 is that trace for windows/amd64.

